#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198088 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Are Attained on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "ed6483ae-971a-543b-adb6-72ac5809b602" :question/math-academy-id "e-12662" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})= -x_1^2+2x_2^2+6x_3^2+4x_1x_2,$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1,$ is equal to $6.$ Find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the maximum is attained." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/e2/e207d971031273136f2c8ddc4e4f4ea462a4f7d8aa9c893b0973101a0e2e24f4.svg)\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/ea/eab2969eff48fccb1fc2c4e91181e83b71707f80d1e315e07293aeb5b9011704.svg)   \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is already normalized ($\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|=1$), we conclude that,   ![](images/98/98be1156a6de649bcd1aee6f38b2c71447089e477b50150795517d5704e3fa25.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198089 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "8a1adb2a-e9c5-52e1-bfa0-6977884fae42" :question/math-academy-id "e-12663" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=9x_1^2-24x_1x_2+16x_2^2,$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1,$ is equal to $0.$ Find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the minimum is attained." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/da/da2ae479bd2bb629add35ffd9f0a66bc96256ad380b7f61789893d9bc9be6ef0.svg)\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/95/955f504032fc42c502d4cd1bb8f68645930cdf2b4a08a48e80eea2ac6214acc9.svg)  \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = 5,$ we get \n\n![](images/2f/2f70f885f2773dc48443efba4c673d6d19f443b4fc1ad8f135e2cab95fad21ea.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198090 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17fd5c3d-d4c6-5f78-b262-fbc6b7209501" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained Over an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "a6abb2f6-6e59-59ac-8549-62d25b3b6e9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12664" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=16x_1^2+16x_1x_2+x_2^2$ given that $16x_1^2 + x_2^2=16,$ and find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the minimum is obtained." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n![](images/ef/ef4c1994a6c3980934ab8b95a92be32293c434fa84a0ffb70dc82cf3198367b2.svg)\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x_1^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{x_2^2}{4^2}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=1$ (along the $x_1$-axis) and $b=4$ (along the $x_2$-axis). \n\nIf we introduce new variables\n\n$\\qquad$ $y_1={x_1} \\quad$ and $\\quad y_2 = \\dfrac{x_2}{4},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into $y_1^2+y_2^2 = 1,$ or $\\|\\mathbf{y}\\|=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n![](images/df/dfa724469e02e9851ec5dc895fe7dda810836745d3a1781a8c60f01d09fbd32b.svg) \n\nwe obtain that $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = (P\\mathbf{y})^T A \\, (P\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{y}^T \\! (P^TAP) \\mathbf{y}= Q'(\\mathbf{y}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $P^T=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 \\\\ 32 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimise $Q'(\\mathbf{y})$ when $\\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}16-\\lambda  & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (16-\\lambda )(16-\\lambda )-1\\,024 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-32\\lambda -768 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -48)(\\lambda +16) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =48,\\,-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: | \\: \\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}} = -16.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=-16{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-16I) & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{aligned}]+16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}32 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 32\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-16I))\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/49/49d16ecc1b8c785d665bd961f24f5627f18fceec2a74dee5b2b1f4c39f2864a8.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/18/18d7c92aca6dc096a79cf0ae8ea6ef99e058da58481689af353f430e726b69a5.svg) \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3e58ec-a069-5c04-b3f3-191e368fce34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266375} {:answer/id #uuid "26450e7f-99ef-532b-a5bd-fc05e3212848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266486} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf29a7d-c26f-5e2d-aea5-c1086ff7611f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266534} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb1a41e-e722-5153-a1d7-614c622c72b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266558} {:answer/id #uuid "315990ad-db42-56b4-b470-e9bc2c220bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b3e58ec-a069-5c04-b3f3-191e368fce34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266375} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ecba40d-5f78-569a-ba4e-84129b21bab3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0419637b-40c5-5740-b2f6-97e085201516" :question/math-academy-id "q-136827" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $5.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb041804-f145-50b4-816c-fcab9c17bcdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388519} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e13d89-d82e-50de-b738-ce617b4eb4c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388520} {:answer/id #uuid "4da080a5-de45-50af-a433-06854c96c579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388521} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2330d3-d405-54f2-a9a6-48d3b6275ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388522} {:answer/id #uuid "3e679990-9d14-53c0-a924-3aa2912773d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e2330d3-d405-54f2-a9a6-48d3b6275ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388522} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c95db7-7a90-557c-8b94-0b154d58f86d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e8e756a-b2ab-5c3c-9c14-7a7f8b61663e" :question/math-academy-id "q-138085" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62a6d5f1-07de-5c5d-8398-135e03352f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{20}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267011} {:answer/id #uuid "77019ed7-47fe-5d52-982c-ad34ce8562bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267188} {:answer/id #uuid "85f9f46b-dc8c-592b-a59f-a5b84b64441a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267325} {:answer/id #uuid "a50456f6-7cbc-5cc0-9fb5-0e45d0022ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267631} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0828c2-83a4-5bb3-becb-3137f80bc0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a50456f6-7cbc-5cc0-9fb5-0e45d0022ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267631} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ab36119-acc0-5be9-8df1-ce5162907a11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6aad317f-209e-580e-8101-2d395c89970f" :question/math-academy-id "q-138086" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-1.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1))x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Are Attained on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "659e6953-7cee-5b96-970f-fa397b86ee27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388526} {:answer/id #uuid "326d7edd-18b0-5a3b-b08d-0b77a365bbd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388527} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f8657c-b325-5796-aed4-2807063c509c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388528} {:answer/id #uuid "aeba8346-f317-5cca-9056-2ca0f6aec458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388529} {:answer/id #uuid "76dc6aea-81a8-5e26-8056-6496647263c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76dc6aea-81a8-5e26-8056-6496647263c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388530} :answer-field/id #uuid "43aa64d2-1826-583b-afa1-0a5a086f07b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aacc9ef4-d33c-5e64-bc37-708eaf7597fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-138090" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $1.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nSo, we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Are Attained on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1512bf0f-a0b6-5737-8456-15bf7348d13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414668} {:answer/id #uuid "189d044b-20ef-594f-9050-30d36ea1c805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414669} {:answer/id #uuid "3cba96ef-b9b9-582c-9ce3-fb19b45e7859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414670} {:answer/id #uuid "b75669b6-5915-5577-8d99-85ede9b169dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414671} {:answer/id #uuid "e53e0f0c-01ad-5114-93c5-0442b0a0663a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1512bf0f-a0b6-5737-8456-15bf7348d13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414668} :answer-field/id #uuid "72e392f4-71c8-5797-8085-77f0d25ff7b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48e09a9b-efe0-5a6d-96f4-c82dbcd33806" :question/math-academy-id "q-138095" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $3.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17fd5c3d-d4c6-5f78-b262-fbc6b7209501" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained Over an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "084f7ce5-201e-5ad6-9bce-d9e103302f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281759} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0ebcf9-e9a6-5cca-97f6-85bfa563e4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281926} {:answer/id #uuid "b4cebb42-cb22-5ab6-8b45-cbd08c48d13e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283255} {:answer/id #uuid "c2dcb565-f7cc-56d6-babf-2810305e6f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283387} {:answer/id #uuid "dd543f2a-9082-5f1c-b191-79aeac5df263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4cebb42-cb22-5ab6-8b45-cbd08c48d13e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283255} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7dce51-5a8d-500a-a178-44f5571990b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "38171cb4-52a5-5ebe-b692-159c5b30da34" :question/math-academy-id "q-170138" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=36,$ is $40.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $40,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{max}=40.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=40:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-40I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{aligned}]-40[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-40I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "532e6508-61ba-5eba-82b1-83db21c9fb33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\e